title: Writing Journal 1 — Who Am I As a Writer?
authors: Writing Journal 1 SBA, built in lo-blocks
description: A first-year ENGL 1010 student, unsure they count as a writer at all, is invited by Alma to the Writers' Circle, which meets in person on the library's second floor. The student arrives mid-conversation, meets Lee and Richard, learns the group's habit of describing themselves by "writer orientation," watches Alma talk through her own Writer Board, then builds their own (what they write and how often), works out why they write each kind, and finally reads the Circle's deck of eight writer-type cards and chooses one — then has to defend the choice with a story from their own writing life.
~~~~

The Invitation
--------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_welcome

You: hey — you took 1010 last year right [face=smile]

You: what did you even do for the "who am i as a writer" journal. i've been staring at it all week

Alma: ha. i stared at it for three days [face=cheeky]

Alma: come to Writers' Circle on Thursday — 4pm, library, second floor [face=smile]

Alma: it's just students who get together every so often to talk about writing. what they're working on, what's been tripping them up. no pressure, come see [face=calm]

Thursday, 4:02
--------------

Lee: —and I didn't figure out what the paper was even about until I was two pages in. which is every time. that's just how it goes. [face=cheeky]

Richard: you say that like it's news. that's been every paper since last fall. [face=serious]

Lee: it's a process. [face=cheeky]

Richard: it's the absence of one. [face=serious]

Alma: okay, be nice — we've got a guest. everyone, this is our friend from Ellington's 1010 this semester. [face=smile]

Richard: ah. so you got the "who am I as a writer" journal. [face=serious]

You: yeah. I've been kind of stuck on it. [face=calm]

Lee: everyone's stuck on that one. it's a strange question the first time somebody asks it out loud. [face=concerned]

The Introductions
-----------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_orientations

Alma: actually — before anything, we should do the introductions properly. not names. the other thing. [face=explaining]

Richard: there's a set of writer types this group uses. nobody takes them too seriously. they're a shorthand. [face=serious]

Alma: the whole shorthand just came up over on the right ---> [face=smile]

Alma: I'm a community writer 🤝 — which mostly means everything I write is pointed at another person. [face=smile]

Lee: explorer 🧭. I write to find out what I think about things. [face=cheeky]

Richard: technician 🔧. I write so things get done properly the first time. [face=serious]

Lee: you don't have to pick one this second, by the way. there's a whole deck of them. you take one at the end. [face=cheeky]

You: I pick my own? [face=calm]

Alma: you pick your own. we used to guess for people — Richard would read your board and hand you a card. [face=explaining]

Richard: and then you'd tell me where I was wrong, which was the useful part. [face=serious]

Richard: this is the honest version of that. you choose, and then you say what makes you sure. harder, and it's yours. [face=serious]

Richard: read it properly. there's a **Mark as read** at the bottom for when you have. [face=serious]

--- wait @wj1_orientations_done.value ---

The Board Comes Up
------------------

Lee: oh — Richard, the thing I was going to tell you. that assignment I turned in Tuesday? I was completely stuck on the opening. sat there an hour. [face=concerned]

Richard: and? [face=serious]

Lee: and I pulled up my writer board for some reason, and I'd forgotten I had "long replies in group chats" on there. which is exactly what the opening needed to sound like — someone explaining something to a person they already know. wrote it like that, took twenty minutes. [face=awe]

Richard: that is the first genuinely useful thing that board has done for anybody. [face=serious]

Lee: it's bailed me out plenty of times, actually. [face=cheeky]

Alma: wait — have you ever made a writer board? [face=explaining]

You: I don't think so. what is it? [face=calm]

Alma: oh, then this is good timing. here — it's faster if I just show you mine. [face=smile]

wj1_sidebar <- wj1_alma_board

Alma: there it is, on the right ---> [face=smile]

Alma: so it's just everything you write, laid out. mine's — okay. texting, obviously. every day, outside school. the roommate group chat, which is ninety percent negotiating whose turn it is to buy paper towels. [face=explaining]

Richard: gripping. [face=serious]

Alma: it's still writing. emails go on the school side — professors, work, a couple times a week. discussion posts, weekly, and I resent every one of them. captions when I post something. comments on shared docs when we're doing group projects, which honestly adds up to a lot of— [face=explaining]

Alma: huh. I always forget how much ends up on here. [face=awe]

Lee: everyone forgets. [face=cheeky]

Alma: no, but the first time I did this I put down three things. essays, emails, texting. I thought that was the list. [face=concerned]

Richard: and now? [face=serious]

Alma: now it doesn't fit on one screen. [face=smile]

Lee: don't worry, there's no quiz on Alma's paper-towel negotiations. hit **Mark as read** whenever you've seen enough [face=cheeky]

--- wait @wj1_alma_board_done.value ---

Your Turn — Build Your Board
----------------------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_board

Alma: okay, your turn. it doesn't have to be complete or organized — just put down what you actually do. there's a side for school and a side for everything else. [face=smile]

Lee: and count the stuff you don't think counts. that's the whole trick. [face=cheeky]

Alma: no rush at all. **Mark as complete** is down at the bottom for whenever you're ready [face=calm]

--- wait @wj1_board_done.value ---

There It Is
-----------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_board_noticing

Lee: there it is. [face=cheeky]

Alma: okay, real question — did anything end up on there that you wouldn't have called writing before you sat down? there's a spot on the right. [face=explaining]

Richard: that's the part that gets everybody. I had grocery lists on mine for about a year before I'd admit they counted. [face=serious]

Lee: they don't count. [face=cheeky]

Richard: they absolutely count. [face=serious]

wj1_board_ready <- go

Alma: oh — and your whole board just saved itself up top under **My Journal**. you can peek at the whole thing in one spot whenever. [face=smile]

Does That Even Count?
---------------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_blank

Richard: for the record — the grocery list thing. that's still writing. [face=serious]

Lee: Richard. it's a list of nouns. [face=cheeky]

Richard: it's a list of nouns that does a job. [face=serious]

Lee: by that standard my phone's autocomplete is a writer. [face=cheeky]

Alma: okay, but how would you two ever settle it? you're both looking at the same word on the same board. nothing on there tells either of you who's right. [face=explaining]

Richard: …no, it does, though. not the item. the reason. I write a list so the thing gets done. if you wrote the exact same list because it makes your head quieter — that's not the same activity. it just looks the same from outside. [face=thinking]

Lee: huh. [face=awe]

Richard: don't do that. [face=serious]

Lee: no — that's why the board actually worked for me on Tuesday. I didn't use "long replies in group chats." I used the fact that I write those to explain something to someone I already know. that was the part I needed. the entry on its own would've told me nothing. [face=thinking]

Alma: which is the thing about a board. any board. it's a list of what. it can't tell you whether any of it counts, or what a single piece of it is for. [face=explaining]

Alma: so — go back through yours and put the why on it. [face=smile]

wj1_sidebar <- wj1_why

--- wait @wj1_why_reflect.value && @wj1_why_top2.value.length === 2 ---

Enough to Run It
----------------

Alma: okay. so now you've got what you write and why you write it. that's enough to go to the deck. [face=smile]

You: what deck? [face=calm]

Lee: the cards. one per writer type. you read through them and you take the one that's you. [face=cheeky]

Lee: took me about nine seconds. explorer. [face=cheeky]

Richard: nine seconds is not reading. you took explorer because it's the card that excuses not outlining. [face=serious]

Lee: and yet. correct. [face=cheeky]

Lee: meanwhile Richard took technician, which stunned this room to its core. [face=cheeky]

Richard: I took it because it's accurate. I read every card first. twice. [face=serious]

Lee: he did read them all twice. I watched him do it. [face=cheeky]

Richard: which is the actual advice. read all of them. people stop at the first card that flatters them and call that a decision. [face=serious]

Alma: mine took me a while — pragmatist was close, honestly. what settled it was remembering one specific night I rewrote a message to my sister four times before I sent it. [face=calm]

Alma: that's the part that matters. not which card. the thing you remember that makes you sure. [face=smile]

You: what if none of them are me? [face=calm]

Alma: then you write one. there's a blank card at the back for exactly that. [face=smile]

Lee: somebody made every card in that deck, you know. they didn't come down off a mountain. [face=cheeky]

Richard: the shorthand belongs to whoever's using it. that includes you now. [face=serious]

Your Writer Type
----------------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_profile
wj1_types_ready <- go

Alma: the first card's just the cover — the arrows move you through the deck. there it is, on the right ---> [face=smile]

Richard: **This one's me** is the button, on the card. and you can change it after — nothing locks. [face=serious]

Lee: oh, and the whole list is up under the **Writer Types** tab now if you want them side by side. [face=cheeky]

--- wait @wj1_type_choice.value ---

Lee: that one? okay, I can see it. [face=cheeky]

Richard: now the part that's actually work. you chose it — so you can defend it. tell the story. [face=serious]

Richard: a real one. what you were writing, what happened, what it took. a card can't hold a story. that's the entire reason we ask for one. [face=serious]

Alma: and if it only fits you three-quarters of the way, say that in the story too. the quarter that doesn't fit is usually the interesting part. [face=calm]

--- wait @wj1_profile_reflect.value ---

Wrapping Up
-----------
wj1_sidebar <- wj1_wrapup

Alma: here's the thing to take with you — none of this is a verdict. it's a snapshot of what you write and why, today. the writing you're about to do in this class will keep changing the picture. [face=calm]

Alma: your whole journal is gathered under **Print &amp; Submit**, up top — the button on the right will take you. give it a read, click the confirm box when you're happy, and export the PDF to hand in. really glad you came. [face=smile]
